:R-HSA-70326  a             obo:GO_0006006 , owl:NamedIndividual ;
        rdfs:comment        "Glucose is the major form in which dietary sugars are made available to cells of the human body. Its breakdown is a major source of energy for all cells, and is essential for the brain and red blood cells. Glucose utilization begins with its uptake by cells and conversion to glucose 6-phosphate, which cannot traverse the cell membrane. Fates open to cytosolic glucose 6-phosphate include glycolysis to yield pyruvate, glycogen synthesis, and the pentose phosphate pathway. In some tissues, notably the liver and kidney, glucose 6-phosphate can be synthesized from pyruvate by the pathway of gluconeogenesis." ;
        rdfs:label          "Glucose metabolism" ;
        dc:contributor      "GOC:reactome_curators" , "Edited: D'Eustachio, P, 0000-00-00 00:00:00" , "Authored: Schmidt, EE, 2003-02-05 00:00:00" ;
        dc:date             "2023-06-21" ;
        pav:providedBy      "https://reactome.org" ;
